<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A bus company needs <m>{430}</m> hours of driving each week on its long-distance routes. Its safety rule is that no driver drives more than <m>{8}</m> hours in a day, or on more than <m>{5}</m> days in a week.</p><p>(a) What is the most one driver may drive in a week?</p><p><fillin characters="6" name="ans1"/> hours  </p><p>(b) What is the least number of drivers the company needs?</p><p><fillin characters="6" name="ans2"/> drivers  </p><p>(c) The company lowers the daily limit to <m>{6}</m> hours. What is the least number of drivers now?</p><p><fillin characters="6" name="ans3"/> drivers  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) One driver may drive at most <m>{8} \times {5} = {40}</m> hours a week.</p><p>(b) <m>{430} \div {40} = {\frac{43}{4}} \approx {10.8}</m>. Drivers come whole, so round up: <m>{10}</m> drivers give only <m>{400}</m> hours, less than <m>{430}</m>, so the company needs <m>{11}</m> drivers.</p><p>(c) Now each driver may drive <m>{6} \times {5} = {30}</m> hours a week. <m>{430} \div {30} \approx {14.3}</m>, so the company needs <m>{15}</m> drivers (<m>{14}</m> drivers give only <m>{420}</m> hours).</p><p>A lower limit means more drivers and a higher wage bill, but rested drivers are safer for passengers and for the drivers themselves.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>40</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>11</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>15</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>